\details{
The ranked probability score (\code{rps}) is
  \eqn{\sum_{j=1}^{K-1}\{F_j-I(Y\leq j)\}^2};
  \code{rps_normalized} divides by \eqn{K-1}, and lies between zero and one.
  \code{log_loss} is \eqn{-\log p_Y}. All are losses: smaller values are
  better. Grouped rows average the individual-outcome losses, rather than
  squaring differences from grouped outcome proportions. Overall scores
  average over the included individual frequency. Log probabilities are
  evaluated directly without clipping. A positive count in a category with
  structural probability zero has infinite log loss; a zero count contributes
  zero. Extremely small positive probabilities can have a finite log loss
  even when the displayed probability underflows to zero.

  Missing outcomes, weights, predictors, or offsets, and zero-frequency rows,
  are retained in \code{rows} with missing scores and an exclusion reason.
  Infinite predictors or offsets are errors. Empty calibration bins remain
  in the table with zero frequency and missing means. With no usable outcomes,
  overall scores are missing. Bins include their lower boundary; the last bin
  also includes one. Fitting rows omitted under \code{na.exclude}
  are restored when assessing the original response. Explicit responses with
  no \code{newdata} must match the retained design rows.

  Scores on the fitting outcomes describe apparent performance. Supplying
  held-out outcomes evaluates the frozen model; the function does not establish
  that those outcomes were excluded during fitting. The optional binary fits
  use the supplied assessment outcomes and design when supplied. They affect
  only the proportional-odds display, never predictions or scores. The dashed
  reference slopes always come from the original ordinal model.
  Positive slopes correspond to higher outcomes, matching the ordinal model.
  The binary estimates are correlated, and bias reduction can produce different
  finite-sample changes at each threshold. Their separate standard errors are
  descriptive; neither interval overlap nor this plot is a formal test of
  proportional odds. Thresholds with no outcome variation or unsuccessful
  fits retain their status and have unavailable estimates. A threshold is also
  unavailable when binary count conversion would round a positive minority
  frequency away (\code{unrepresentable_binary_counts}). Calibration alone
  is also not a test of proportional odds.

  With \code{po.test="score"}, a common-slope null on the fitted link is refitted to
  the included assessment outcomes, predictors, frequencies, and offsets.
  Maximum likelihood is the default, including when \code{object} used
  mean bias reduction. Set \code{po.estimation="meanBR"} to request a
  mean-bias-reduced null explicitly. The resulting ordinary efficient score
  retains its nuisance projection and has only first-order justification;
  it can be conservative in small samples. The null refit
  affects neither the original model nor the prediction scores. With
  supplied outcomes, the test concerns the common-slope restriction in those
  outcomes, rather than calibration of the original model's coefficients.
  The score uses expected Fisher information, averaged analytically over
  possible outcomes under this null, and projects out the threshold,
  common-slope, and any untested varying-effect nuisance scores. No
  resampling is used for its p-value.

  If \code{s} design columns are tested across \code{K} categories, the
  asymptotic chi-square reference has \code{s*(K-2)} degrees of freedom.
  For a common-slope fit, other predictors retain common slopes in both the
  null and the local alternative. For a partial fit, other varying effects
  remain unrestricted. The latter uses the term-equality construction in
  \code{\link{fastglm_po_test}}. The descriptive binary-slope comparison is
  available only for common-slope fits. Independent observations and a
  correctly specified, identifiable interior null are required. The
  approximation can be inaccurate in small or sparse samples; a large
  p-value does not establish proportional odds. The test is not a robust,
  cluster-adjusted, or higher-order bias-corrected test.

  The score test uses dense data or a repeatable source, at least three declared
  categories, at least one tested predictor, and positive frequency in every
  category. Empty categories, deficient rank, failed null fits, and
  unavailable information produce missing statistics and p-values with an
  explicit reason. Categories and degrees of freedom are not silently
  reduced, and the calculation does not switch to a mean bias-reduced fit.
  The descriptive slope comparison and formal test can be requested
  independently. Formula term selection requires retained design assignments
  from a current ordinal fit, including when \code{model=FALSE}.

  Scoring and calibration use blocks of rows and require work proportional to
  the number of rows times the number of categories. The optional binary
  comparison fits one model per threshold without expanding frequency rows.
  The score test also processes frequencies without expanding observations;
  its information matrix grows with the predictor and category dimensions.

  For an ordinal source fit with no supplied evaluation data, scoring and
  calibration reread the recorded source once. The same frequency, offset,
  category, and prediction calculations as dense assessment are used.
  The source must return unchanged rows and values. Weights and offsets
  cannot be replaced in this mode. Summary storage depends on the number of
  categories and bins; row calculations use the current source block.
  \code{keep.rows=TRUE} collects the row table, while \code{sink} can save
  it block by block. Source assessment describes apparent performance on
  the fitting outcomes. The optional common-slope score test requires
  additional source passes for refitting and information accumulation.
  Descriptive binary-slope comparisons still require explicit evaluation data.

